WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.165
Go's concurrency model is one of its
strengths, but concurrency still needs design.

2
00:00:04.245 --> 00:00:07.083
A goroutine should have an owner, a reason
to exist, an error path,

3
00:00:07.163 --> 00:00:07.886
and a way to stop.

4
00:00:07.966 --> 00:00:10.702
Unbounded goroutines, missing
cancellation, blocked sends,

5
00:00:10.782 --> 00:00:14.188
and forgotten workers can turn a clean
service into one that leaks resources or

6
00:00:14.268 --> 00:00:15.817
behaves unpredictably under load.

7
00:00:15.897 --> 00:00:18.632
context.Context is an important tool for
defensive services.

8
00:00:18.712 --> 00:00:21.035
Context can carry deadlines, cancellation
signals,

9
00:00:21.115 --> 00:00:24.212
and request scope through handlers,
database calls, service clients,

10
00:00:24.292 --> 00:00:25.221
and background work.

11
00:00:25.301 --> 00:00:27.159
It should not become a general storage
bag,

12
00:00:27.239 --> 00:00:30.542
but it should make cancellation and time
limits visible as work moves across

13
00:00:30.622 --> 00:00:31.190
boundaries.

14
00:00:31.270 --> 00:00:32.457
Channels need clear rules.

15
00:00:32.537 --> 00:00:34.911
Teams should know who sends, who receives,
who closes,

16
00:00:34.991 --> 00:00:38.037
whether buffering is intentional, and what
happens during shutdown.

17
00:00:38.117 --> 00:00:41.162
Ambiguous channel ownership can lead to
blocked work, missed errors,

18
00:00:41.242 --> 00:00:42.377
or panics during cleanup.

19
00:00:42.457 --> 00:00:45.554
The safest channel designs are simple
enough to explain during review.

20
00:00:45.634 --> 00:00:47.286
Shared state needs synchronization.

21
00:00:47.366 --> 00:00:50.153
Mutexes, atomic values, channel ownership,
immutable copies,

22
00:00:50.233 --> 00:00:53.433
and single-writer designs can all be
reasonable when used deliberately.

23
00:00:53.513 --> 00:00:56.713
Race conditions can become more than
reliability issues when they affect

24
00:00:56.793 --> 00:01:00.252
authorization state, cached configuration,
session data, counters, limits,

25
00:01:00.332 --> 00:01:01.364
or security decisions.

26
00:01:01.444 --> 00:01:04.592
Graceful shutdown protects data integrity
and operational stability.

27
00:01:04.672 --> 00:01:06.479
A service should stop accepting new work,

28
00:01:06.559 --> 00:01:08.882
cancel or finish in-flight work according
to policy,

29
00:01:08.962 --> 00:01:11.439
flush important logs or metrics, and
release resources.

30
00:01:11.519 --> 00:01:15.029
Concurrency is secure when its lifecycle
is bounded, observable, and testable.

31
00:01:15.109 --> 00:01:18.257
The review question is simple: if this
goroutine starts, who can stop it,

32
00:01:18.337 --> 00:01:20.660
who sees its error, and what data remains
consistent?
